# Mercury Agent — deployment-image accepted-risk ledger (machine-enforced).
#
# This file is consumed by the BLOCKING Trivy gates in
# .github/workflows/ci.yml and .github/workflows/security.yml, which run
# with `ignore-unfixed: false`. Every entry below is therefore an explicit,
# enumerated, expiring acceptance — when an entry expires (exp:) the gate
# starts failing and forces a re-review; a NEW unfixed CRITICAL/HIGH
# finding that is not listed here blocks the merge instead of passing
# silently. This replaces the previous blanket `ignore-unfixed: true`
# posture, which waived the entire unfixed-OS-CVE class invisibly.
#
# Rules for this file:
#   * Entries require owner review; record component, reason, and source.
#   * Only no-upstream-fix OS CVEs whose package is genuinely irreducible
#     belong here. If a package can be removed, it is removed from the
#     Dockerfile (eliminate, don't accept) — see the perl-base / mesa notes.
#   * Anything with a fixed version available is remediated by the
#     Dockerfile's runtime-stage `apt-get upgrade -y` and must never be listed.
#   * Expiry is 90 days (the documented quarterly review cadence).
#
# Enumeration source: the blocking gates' own scan of the BUILT image
# (trivy 0.70.0 via aquasecurity/trivy-action@v0.36.0 — the version the
# enforcing workflows actually run), re-enumerated 2026-06-30 against the
# shipped Debian **trixie** base (`python:3.14-slim-trixie`, Debian 13.5)
# after the Python 3.13 -> 3.14 base-image bump, the base-image migration off
# bookworm, and the perl-base elimination below; re-checked 2026-07-02 after
# the vulnerability DB published 4 new unfixed CRITICAL/HIGH findings against
# the 2026-06-30 posture (gzip, libglib2.0-0t64 — both ELIMINATED below, not
# accepted). RE-REVIEWED 2026-09-27 (Debian 13.7) after the scheduled
# Security run went red on the expired ncurses entry plus five new unfixed
# HIGH findings (util-linux x4, systemd x1): the runtime stage's OS layer was
# rebuilt and scanned with trivy 0.70.0, which reproduced the CI failure
# 1:1, and each finding was cross-checked against the Debian Security
# Tracker. RE-REVIEWED 2026-11-01 (Debian 13.7) after the Docker Build gate
# went red between two pushes on the same day with no
# Dockerfile or dependency change: the vulnerability DB had published two new
# unfixed HIGH findings against libsqlite3-0 (CVE-2026-119373,
# CVE-2026-119586), reproduced 1:1 by the security.yml table-format scan
# (run 41335629579) and cross-checked against the Debian Security Tracker
# (vulnerable in bookworm, trixie, forky and sid; the upstream fix is SQLite
# 3.54.0, which no Debian suite ships). RE-REVIEWED 2026-11-05 (Debian 13.7)
# after the blocking gate went red on a newly published zlib1g HIGH
# (CVE-2026-76844) between a green run on main at 06:27Z and a push at
# 20:17Z with no Dockerfile or dependency change: reproduced with the
# CI-pinned trivy 0.70.0 against a Debian 13.7 dpkg manifest and
# cross-checked against the Debian Security Tracker and NVD. The built
# runtime image now carries 13 distinct unfixed CRITICAL/HIGH OS CVEs
# (0 CRITICAL, 13 HIGH); none has a fixed version published for trixie and
# each package is a hard dependency of CPython, bash, coreutils/tar or
# apt/dpkg. RE-REVIEWED 2026-11-06 after the Security workflow on main went
# red on three newly published HIGH findings (libsqlite3-0 x2, zlib1g x1)
# against an unchanged Dockerfile. The same day all five zlib1g/libsqlite3-0
# findings were REMEDIATED rather than accepted (Debian's fixed builds are
# installed in place; see "FIXED IN PLACE" below), and the inert
# CVE-2026-119586 entry was dropped; the acl and systemd acceptances were
# remediated the same way. The ledger now holds 5 entries (0 CRITICAL,
# 5 HIGH): ncurses x1 and util-linux x4. Both are fixed in Debian unstable,
# but neither fixed build is installable on trixie -- each requires
# libc6 >= 2.42 and trixie ships 2.41 -- so curing them in place would mean
# replacing the C library itself, which this ledger does not do.
#
# DROPPED in the 2026-09-27 re-review (not carried inert):
#   * CVE-2026-53615 (util-linux libblkid) — FIXED in trixie
#     2.41.5-0+deb13u1, which the runtime stage's `apt-get upgrade` installs;
#     the scan no longer reports it.
#   * CVE-2026-54371 (libattr1) — re-scored HIGH -> MEDIUM by the vendor;
#     still unfixed in trixie 1:2.5.2-3 but out of scope for the
#     CRITICAL/HIGH blocking gate. Re-add here, don't silently drop, if a
#     future re-scoring raises it back to HIGH.
#
# ELIMINATED, not accepted (removed from the image rather than waived here):
#   * perl-base (CVE-2026-8376, CVE-2026-42496 [Critical]; CVE-2026-42497,
#     CVE-2026-48962, CVE-2026-9538 [High]) — a Debian "essential" package
#     with no runtime use: Mercury runs no Perl, and the non-root user is
#     created with `useradd`, not `adduser`. The Dockerfile purges perl-base +
#     adduser right after `apt-get upgrade`, so all 5 CVEs are gone from the
#     image. This removed both of the ledger's former CRITICALs (8 → 3).
#   * mesa GL stack (CVE-2026-40393) — see the note at the foot of this file.
#   * libglib2.0-0 / libglib2.0-0t64 (CVE-2026-58016 [Critical];
#     CVE-2026-58014, CVE-2026-58015 [High], 2026-07-02) — was installed only
#     as cv2's historical libgthread-2.0 import dependency, but the shipped
#     opencv-python-headless wheel (>= 4.13) vendors its media stack and
#     links no glib library at all (verified: readelf on the wheel's
#     cv2.abi3.so NEEDED list; cv2 import + cvtColor/Canny run clean on a
#     glib-less trixie base). The Dockerfile no longer installs it, so all
#     3 CVEs are gone from the image rather than waived here.
#   * gzip (CVE-2026-41992 [High], 2026-07-02) — Debian "essential" but with
#     no runtime consumer: CPython's gzip/zlib modules use the linked libz
#     (never the gzip binary), dpkg/apt decompress with internal code, and
#     the image never runs `tar -z`. The Dockerfile purges it alongside
#     perl-base (verified post-purge: apt-get update/install/upgrade, dpkg,
#     python gzip round-trip all work), so the CVE is gone from the image.
#
# DROPPED in the 2026-06-30 trixie re-enumeration (present in the prior
# ledger; not carried inert, because each is gone or no longer a CRITICAL/HIGH
# finding on trixie): CVE-2026-11822 / CVE-2026-11824 (libsqlite3-0 FTS5
# bugs — re-scored by the upstream vendor from HIGH to MEDIUM since the
# 2026-06-18 enumeration; still present in 3.46.1-7+deb13u1 with no upstream
# fix, but now out of scope for the CRITICAL/HIGH blocking gate. Re-add here,
# don't silently drop, if a future re-scoring raises them back to HIGH).
#
# Common mitigations (every remaining entry): the container runs as non-root
# UID 1000 with SUID/SGID bits stripped, and none of the vulnerable code sits
# on an untrusted-input path in the shipped API. Debian trixie publishes no
# patched build for any of them to `apt-get upgrade` to. Each is accepted
# only as a time-boxed, enumerated, expiring risk — never silenced.

# ncurses (libncursesw6 / libtinfo6 / ncurses-base / ncurses-bin
# 6.5+20250216-2) — Debian trixie `<no-dsa>` (Minor issue), fixed only in
# forky/sid (upstream 6.5-20251213). The overflow is in the infocmp(1)
# command-line tool (progs/infocmp.c analyze_string), not in the shared
# libraries; Mercury never runs infocmp. The libraries are linked by bash
# (libtinfo6) and CPython's readline/_curses stdlib modules, so the package
# family cannot be removed without breaking the shell and the interpreter.
# Re-reviewed 2026-09-27 (previous acceptance expired 2026-09-16).
CVE-2025-69720 exp:2026-12-26

# util-linux family (bsdutils, libblkid1, liblastlog2-2, libmount1,
# libsmartcols1, libuuid1, login, util-linux 2.41.5-0+deb13u1) — four HIGH
# findings published after the 2026-07-09 review, all Debian trixie
# `<no-dsa>` (Minor issue), fixed only in forky/sid (2.42.3 / 2.42.4):
#   * CVE-2026-76642, CVE-2026-78409, CVE-2026-78410 — SUID mount(8)
#     X-mount.* post-hook / X-mount.subdir / restricted-bind-mount flaws that
#     let an unprivileged user with an fstab-authorised entry redirect a
#     privileged mount. The `mount` package (mount/umount/losetup/swapon) is
#     PURGED from the runtime image as of 2026-09-27 (it has no reverse
#     dependency and the container never mounts anything), so the vulnerable
#     entry point is gone; the libmount1 library remains only because
#     util-linux's own tools link it.
#   * CVE-2026-78408 — nsenter --join-cgroup leaks a root cgroup.procs fd; it
#     requires a *privileged operator* to run nsenter against an
#     attacker-controlled target. The container runs no nsenter and has no
#     root operator inside it.
# util-linux and bsdutils are Debian "essential"; libuuid1/libblkid1 back
# CPython and coreutils, so the family itself is irreducible. Added
# 2026-09-27.
CVE-2026-76642 exp:2026-12-26
CVE-2026-78408 exp:2026-12-26
CVE-2026-78409 exp:2026-12-26
CVE-2026-78410 exp:2026-12-26

# FIXED IN PLACE, not accepted (2026-11-06) -- zlib1g, libsqlite3-0, libacl1,
# libsystemd0, libudev1. Eight entries that stood in this file were removed,
# with the reason for each:
#   * CVE-2026-76844, CVE-2026-85091 (zlib gz* layer); CVE-2026-119373,
#     CVE-2026-119374, CVE-2026-119379 (SQLite FTS5 / session extension);
#     CVE-2026-54369 (acl symlink traversal); CVE-2026-16742 (systemd-homed):
#     REMEDIATED. The runtime stage installs Debian's own fixed builds --
#     zlib1g 1:1.3.dfsg+really1.3.2-4, libsqlite3-0 3.54.1-2, libacl1 2.4.0-1,
#     libsystemd0/libudev1 262.2-1, each "fixed" on the Debian tracker and each
#     depending on libc6 alone -- from one pinned, signed snapshot.debian.org
#     timestamp, and the build proves every version. The vulnerable code is no
#     longer in the image.
#     Trivy still reports all seven against the fixed builds (measured with the
#     CI-pinned trivy 0.70.0), because it keys Debian findings on release 13,
#     whose advisory has no fixed version. They are therefore matched by
#     trivy-fixed-in-place.rego, which ignores each one ONLY at the exact fixed
#     version: on trixie's vulnerable builds it ignores nothing (measured), so
#     a regression fails the gate rather than hiding behind this file.
#   * CVE-2026-119586 (sqlite3 CLI `.recover` command injection): DROPPED,
#     not carried inert. The current vulnerability DB does not report it
#     against this image at any severity (Debian status "undetermined"), so
#     the entry suppressed nothing and would only have pre-waived a future
#     re-scoring. The flaw is in the sqlite3 shell, which the image does not
#     install; tests/security/test_fixed_in_place_os_packages.py keeps that
#     true. Re-add here, don't silently drop, if it is reported again.
#
# CORRECTION (2026-11-06), recorded so the earlier reasoning is not re-used:
# the removed 2026-11-05 CVE-2026-76844 entry (in git history and SECURITY.md)
# stated that installing sid's fixed zlib "does not clear the gate", so
# suite-mixing was "ineffective, not merely risky". The
# measurement was right and the conclusion was not. Installing the fixed build
# removes the vulnerability; what it cannot do on its own is change Trivy's
# reading of a release-13 advisory. Binding the scanner's exception to the
# exact fixed version closes that gap without accepting any vulnerable code.

# Mesa GL stack (CVE-2026-40393): REMOVED, not accepted. It was installed
# only as OpenCV's libGL dependency, but Mercury uses
# opencv-python-headless (no libGL linkage) and makes no cv2 GUI calls, so
# the Dockerfile no longer installs libgl1-mesa-glx — the package and its
# CVE are gone from the image. The blocking Trivy gate (ignore-unfixed:
# false) re-verifies this on every build: were the package to reappear,
# the gate would fail rather than silently pass.
